<!-- ============================================================== LIMITS -->
<section id="limits">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--warn)">Known limits</div>
    <h2>The limits, written down<br>before you pay.</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Every generation tool has weak spots. These are EasyFrame&rsquo;s, and you should know
      them now.
    </p>

    <details class="limits-box">
      <summary class="limits-toggle">
        <span class="apri">Show the ten limits</span>
        <span class="chiudi">Hide the limits</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="limits">
      <div class="limit">
        <b>The interface is in Italian</b>
        <p>Menus, buttons and messages are in Italian. Prompts work in English, and the
        prompt-building menus write the English terms for you.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit">
        <b>Text inside images</b>
        <p>It renders text well only when you ask for it explicitly, and best with the
        Qwen-Image bundle, which wants ~24 GB of VRAM. Below that, the cloud is the better choice.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit">
        <b>Crowded scenes on the included model</b>
        <p>FLUX.2 Klein holds up to three figures; from four upwards, at 1216&times;832, faces
        and hands fall apart, because the image runs out of pixels per person. The app spots
        this in the prompt and offers the largest format your card can take. The Krea 2 bundle
        keeps six figures correct at 1024&times;1024, measured, so the warning stays hidden there.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit">
        <b>Complex mechanical shapes</b>
        <p>Bicycles, gears and articulated structures remain the weak spot of a
        4-billion-parameter model.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit">
        <b>Anime presets want tags</b>
        <p>Illustrious is trained on comma-separated tags (1girl, silver hair, rooftop).
        Plain sentences render much worse; the app warns you as you type and the menus write
        the tags for you.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit">
        <b>Five seconds of 720p video</b>
        <p>It needs more than 12 GB of VRAM. The warning never blocks you, and it suggests a
        duration and resolution that actually fit your card.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit">
        <b>Token top-ups are simulated</b>
        <p>In this version the balance is credited without any real payment. Using your own
        API key is complete and fully working.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit">
        <b>The adult presets</b>
        <p>They generate adult subjects only, with an explicit barrier in the negative prompt.
        That barrier is probabilistic; treat it as a safeguard with limits of its own.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit">
        <b>Windows warns on first launch</b>
        <p>The installer is not code-signed yet, so SmartScreen shows &ldquo;Windows protected
        your PC&rdquo;. Click More info, then Run anyway.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit">
        <b>macOS is in beta</b>
        <p>The Apple Silicon build passes the automated tests on macOS, and 0.1.1 fixes the defects
        the first test on a real Mac turned up: window frame, models panel, app icon. Generating with
        ComfyUI on a Mac still isn&rsquo;t verified: some fp8 models may fail to load on Metal. It isn&rsquo;t signed by
        Apple, so the first time you open it from System Settings &rarr; Privacy &amp; Security &rarr;
        Open Anyway, and ComfyUI is installed separately with ComfyUI Desktop.</p>
      </div>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

